This is the ConvoyLink firmware's one allowed external dependency (task
T09). Everything above it — `../sx1262.c` / `../include/sx1262.h` — is
ConvoyLink's own wrapper and is the only API the rest of the firmware may
use.


Local patches
-------------

Kept deliberately small; each is marked in-place with a
`[ConvoyLink patch N/4]` comment so a future re-vendor can find them.

1. ra01s.c — added `#include "convoy_pins.h"`, and replaced the
   `CONFIG_SPI2_HOST`/`CONFIG_SPI3_HOST` conditional with a fixed
   `HOST_ID = SPI3_HOST`.
   Why: docs/02 puts the SX1262 on SPI3, and the pin map is owned by
   convoy_pins.h — not by this driver's Kconfig menu.

2. ra01s.c — `LoRaInit` now takes its eight GPIO numbers from
   `CONVOY_PIN_LORA_*` instead of `CONFIG_*_GPIO`, returns `esp_err_t`
   instead of `void`, and returns that error instead of `assert()`ing when
   `spi_bus_initialize` / `spi_bus_add_device` fail (freeing the bus on the
   second). The eight `ESP_LOGI` lines echoing each CONFIG_ value became
   one line.
   Why: docs/01 requires the radio to fail soft — the app retries every 5 s
   and shows `RADIO?` — so init must never abort the firmware.

3. ra01s.h — `LoRaInit`'s declaration updated to match (2), plus an
   `#include "esp_err.h"` for the return type.

4. ra01s.c — `LoRaSend` arms the modem with `SetTx(200)` rather than
   `SetTx(500)`.
   Why: a 32-byte SF7/125 kHz/4:5 packet is ~61 ms on air (docs/03), and
   this modem timeout is what bounds `LoRaSend`'s synchronous wait loop —
   200 ms is >3x margin and matches the send timeout documented in
   `sx1262.h`.

Not patched, handled in the wrapper instead:

- `LoRaError()` is declared `__attribute__((weak, alias(...)))` over a
  default that loops forever. `sx1262.c` defines a strong replacement and
  uses `setjmp`/`longjmp` to turn an init-time fault into an error return,
  so no edit to the vendored file was needed.
- `Kconfig.projbuild` was not vendored at all: every setting it exposed
  (frequency, SF, BW, CR, pins) is fixed by docs/02 and docs/03 and is
  programmed from `sx1262.c`, so a menuconfig knob for any of them would
  be a second source of truth.
